// RWC (Read-to-Write Causality), with the reader ordered by an address
// dependency and the writer-then-reader ordered by an SC fence.
//
// rmm-zoo property: mca (Atomicity / Multicopy atomic).
// Source: Sarkar et al., "Understanding POWER Multiprocessors" (PLDI 2011) --
//         the RWC family; Alglave et al., "Herding Cats" (TOPLAS 2014).
//
// P0 writes x. P1 reads x=1 and then (address dep) reads y=0. P2 writes y and
// then (fence) reads x=0. Every intra-thread pair that CAN be ordered is
// ordered, yet P1 and P2 disagree about whether W(x,1) has happened.
//
// On POWER the outcome survives because an address dependency is not
// cumulative -- it orders P1's own accesses but says nothing about when P0's
// write propagates elsewhere. That non-cumulativity is precisely the storage
// subsystem's freedom to deliver one write to different threads at different
// times, i.e. the absence of multicopy atomicity. Restoring it needs a full
// sync in P1, not a dependency.
//
// Reference verdicts: SC and x86-TSO FORBID. POWER and ARMv7 (non-MCA) ALLOW.
//   The ARMv8 verdict is the MCA claim of Pulte et al. (POPL 2018) and is cited
//   rather than re-run here; see this directory's entry in ../../README.md.
x := 0;
y := 0;
{
  x := 1
} ||| {
  r1 := x;
  rp := &y;
  r2 := *(rp + (r1 - r1))
} ||| {
  y := 1;
  fence(sc);
  r3 := x
}
%%
allow (r1 = 1 && r2 = 0 && r3 = 0) [Power]
